#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124198 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "454d081a-fdcb-5101-9f85-0e3b27986695" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3961" :question/problem "Which of the graphs below correspond to an invertible function?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/483fa8080bd245ed3dd303ce993001d9.png)" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible if and only if it's a one-to-one function. To determine which of the functions are one-to-one, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/f7e8678ffa8b2c33f49efd58711651f5.png)\n\nFrom the above, we see that:\n\n- In graph IV, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line. Therefore, graph IV shows a one-to-one function. This means that the function is invertible.\n- For graphs I, II, and III, some horizontal lines intersect the curve more than once. So, these are not one-to-one functions. As a result, they are not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124199 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "b6722faa-6069-59c1-8861-75b8207b0a90" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3962" :question/problem "On which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/890bcf1d83358c5e4338a6e24ffa2521.png)\n\n1. $[MATH: (-3,-1]]$\n2. $[MATH: (0,4]]$\n3. $[MATH: [-2,2)]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $[MATH: x\\in (-3,-1].]$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/370f99885675d6ad20854a2868c28fde.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $[MATH: (0,4]]$ and $[MATH: [-2,2)]$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/eb2025e80374eec32f3743673ee7342c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124200 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/id #uuid "7c28a136-0b86-5d13-a5f8-b0dd0e0ea1ba" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-3963" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)= \\mid x-2 \\mid -2$, on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n1. $[MATH: x \\in (-\\infty, 2]]$\n2. $x \\in (0, \\infty)$\n3. $[MATH: x \\in (-1,4]]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's graph the given function. The graph of the function\n\n$$\nf(x)= \\mid x-2 \\mid -2\n$$\n\ncan be obtained in the following way:\n\n1. Take the curve $y= \\mid x \\mid$.\n2. Translate it by $2$ units to the right, to get $y= \\mid x-2 \\mid$.\n3. Finally, translate $y= \\mid x-2 \\mid$ by $2$ units down to get $f(x)= \\mid x-2 \\mid -2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/d0d8a9c6b3405531dde3d55701c507e1.png)\n\nNow, remember that a function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is invertible on the interval $[MATH: x\\in(-\\infty,2].]$ Indeed, on this interval, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/bd8e8f0254d6b5268fc5a78ef6f9bbb4.png)\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not invertible on any of the intervals $x\\in(0,\\infty)$ and $[MATH: x \\in (-1,4].]$ On these intervals, some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/2c1748b44205856a5d6b2aa9d14612ba.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f7d0ca0-7602-5974-a24b-7a49bdb1f021" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122645} {:answer/id #uuid "c1cbe9ce-9211-518e-802c-f018c55edb67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122646} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0bfaea-2fe0-5d38-a426-45d2edb63aca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122647} {:answer/id #uuid "228ec388-ee04-5b76-b68a-f72bf5ac82e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122648} {:answer/id #uuid "4b291071-a48d-5362-a424-8c66a69304af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb0bfaea-2fe0-5d38-a426-45d2edb63aca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122647} :answer-field/id #uuid "704436de-305b-5016-bafe-eed85ac8ef78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122644}] :question/id #uuid "e891cfb3-f978-5b44-b8bf-4d4f9f71b503" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-36941" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1889/Source/Images/q-36941.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n1. $[MATH: x ∈ (-2, 0]]$\n2. $[MATH: x ∈ (-2, 2]]$\n3. $x ∈ [- 4,-2]∪[2, 4]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125111 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8711068c-32aa-5adf-8e3c-3b5bddb4a75d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123689} {:answer/id #uuid "accbf09d-4f3d-5da6-bd81-df5467620a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123690} {:answer/id #uuid "f12a250b-237c-536a-8e99-cddd0504367f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123691} {:answer/id #uuid "5374cfe8-90ce-5552-bcb3-d52e0c0c6a9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123692} {:answer/id #uuid "7c060e43-578e-5d7e-a4e7-bc12db9ea0ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "accbf09d-4f3d-5da6-bd81-df5467620a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123690} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae902fc9-f4cf-51e2-a585-9915ff735075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123688}] :question/id #uuid "9ea11e93-5fca-5cd0-afe6-c972b9a2cce3" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-66482" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = x^{2} + 2$, on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n1. $x ∈ (-2, 2)$\n2. $[MATH: x ∈ [0, ∞)]$\n3. $[MATH: x ∈ (- ∞, 0]]$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "149fa184-850f-52d8-a309-4b79a371a74d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given a Description"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9381b3ca-5691-53f3-8d89-9515548e602e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123695} {:answer/id #uuid "2b014d3a-a4c1-5bec-b5f5-d7189536545c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123696} {:answer/id #uuid "2f97f984-ce2e-5e09-a9ff-2dce19d9e561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123697} {:answer/id #uuid "339391da-486c-5270-a19c-7137c1485049" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123698} {:answer/id #uuid "3d270576-6c58-5c5d-9719-d161aa5919b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123699}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d270576-6c58-5c5d-9719-d161aa5919b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123699} :answer-field/id #uuid "175ed77a-26e6-56ef-93a7-27f91b318f78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123694}] :question/id #uuid "c08528cd-5efd-5572-9af4-e4dc31c682da" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-66723" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = x + 1$, on which of the following intervals is $f(x)$ invertible?\n\n1. $x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$\n2. $x ∈ (-1, ∞)$\n3. $x ∈ (-10, 5)$"}]}
